  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "Income is the money or value received in exchange for labor, services, from the sale of goods, or as profit from financial investments. It is categorized into earned (wages/tips), unearned (interest/dividends/pensions), and business income. Taxed on a gross or net basis, it is crucial for measuring financial health and budgeting.Types of IncomeEarned Income: Money from working, including wages, salaries, tips, and bonuses.Unearned Income: Money received without working, such as interest, dividends, pensions, Social Security, and alimony.Business/Self-Employment Income: Net earnings from selling products or services.Passive Income: Income from property or a business in which you do not actively participate, such as rental income.In-Kind Income: Goods or services received for free or less than fair market value.Sources of IncomeEmployment: Paychecks from employers.Investments: Dividends from stocks, interest from savings accounts, or capital gains.Government Benefits: Social Security, disability, and unemployment payments.Real Estate: Rental income.Retirement Accounts: Pensions, 401(k), and IRA distributions.How Income is CalculatedGross Income: Total income from all sources before taxes and deductions.Adjusted Gross Income (AGI): Gross income minus specific deductions like student loan interest or IRA contributions.Net Income: Income remaining after taxes and expenses are deducted (commonly referred to as \"take-home pay\").How Income is TaxedIncome is generally subject to federal, state, and local taxes.Earned Income: Subject to income tax and payroll taxes (Social Security/Medicare).Unearned Income: Taxed as part of your overall income, sometimes at different rates (e.g., long-term capital gains).Adjustments/Deductions: Certain expenses (e.g., educator expenses, student loan interest) can lower the taxable amount.Role of Income in AccountingProfit Measurement: Revenue minus expenses determines net income (profit).Financial Performance: It determines an entity's ability to pay for expenses, save, and invest.Economic Analysis: Personal income data helps evaluate consumer spending and economic well-being.Note: Certain items, such as child support, veterans' disability payments, and worker's compensation, are generally not considered income.",
